# Contract

What the XMPL100 driver promises, what it needs, and the rules a caller follows.

The driver inherits the [Kokeb Labs driver contract](https://github.com/kokeb-labs/.github/blob/main/standards/driver.md);
this page states only what is specific to it.

## Promises

- The constructor is `noexcept`, does no I/O and cannot fail.
- `Initialize()` configures the pins. With `GpioSet()` it power-cycles the part through SHDN_N and checks the
  self-test pulse on OUT (the closest equivalent of a chip-ID check, since the part has no ID); without it, it waits
  out the pulse of the board's power-up and cannot tell a missing part.
- Every read returns `Error::NotInitialized` before `Initialize()` succeeds.
- Every port failure propagates as `Error::Bus`.
- A reading is never a guess: if the samples of one reading disagree, it is `Error::DeviceFault`.

## Depends on

| Dependency | Version | Why |
| --- | --- | --- |
| A C++20 compiler | GCC 10, Clang 13 or later | Concepts constrain the port |
| CMake | 3.16 or later | Generates `xmpl100_version.h` |
| Your port | | Two GPIO pins, the delay, optionally SHDN_N |

## Threading

Not thread-safe; takes no lock. One owner task, or one mutex around every call. Nothing may run in an ISR: a reading
waits between its samples.

## Errors

| `Error` | Value | Returned when |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `Ok` | 0 | The call succeeded |
| `NotInitialized` | 1 | Before `Initialize()` succeeded |
| `InvalidArgument` | 2 | `Validate()` failed |
| `Bus` | 3 | A pin read or write of the port failed |
| `DeviceIdMismatch` | 5 | No self-test pulse on OUT at `Initialize()` (with `GpioSet()` only) |
| `DeviceFault` | 8 | OUT changed between the samples of one reading |
| `Unsupported` | 9 | `Sleep()` without `GpioSet()` |

`Timeout`, `Crc` and `Busy` are never returned.

## Memory

No heap, exceptions, RTTI or static mutable state. The object holds a port reference, a `Config` and a flag.

## Platform

Platform-free; the ESP-IDF port in `examples/esp32` is an example.

## Deviations from the standard

None.
